#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨1, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(0)) \\\\\n&= f(e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}) - f(1, 0, 1) \\\\\n&= (e(1)^{2} + 2 \\cdot e \\cdot \\frac{1}{e}) - (1(0)^{2} + 2(1)(1)) \\\\\n&= (e + 2) - 2 \\\\\n&= e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, 1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1$.\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nSince we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= ye^{xy}, \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= xe^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨0, 1⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$.\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= f(1, 0) - f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= e^{1 \\cdot 0} - e^{0 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C$. A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1]\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨ - 1, 2⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1)$.\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(-1)) \\\\\n&= f(1, 1) - f(-1, 2) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot e^{1} - (-1) \\cdot e^{2} \\\\\n&= e + e^{2} \\\\\n&= e(1 + e)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fe3e30-c701-50fb-9192-ed3819d71c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130576} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b0acbf-8d9e-57a4-ab15-f6a78d1d18d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value